<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">What is halloumi?</h2>



<p>If you=E2=80=99re a remotely regular reader of the blog, you=E2=80=99ll =
know all about halloumi, since I use it all the time. But just in case you=
=E2=80=99re reading this thinking =E2=80=98what on earth is this halloumi t=
hing?=E2=80=99, I=E2=80=99ll tell you.</p>



<p><a aria-label=3D"Halloumi (opens in a new tab)" href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Halloumi" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer noopener">Hallo=
umi</a> (or haloumi) is a firm cheese from Cyprus. It=E2=80=99s salty and c=
reamy, and basically just insanely delicious.</p>



<p>Unlike most other cheeses, halloumi holds its shape as it cooks, so you =
can grill or fry it without it melting away to nothing. When it=E2=80=99s c=
ooked, it just becomes slightly squidgy and gooey (and you can crisp it up =
beautifully on the edges as well!) =E2=80=93 it=E2=80=99s an irresistible t=
exture.</p>



<p>This makes it perfect for things like curries! You can fry it up along w=
ith your vegetables and it will stay intact, in little pieces that are beau=
tiful to bite into.</p><div id=3D"AdThrive_Content_1_desktop" class=3D"adth=
rive-ad adthrive-sticky-container adthrive-content adthrive-content-1"></di=
v>



